WEBVTT

00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:03.376
Our running example is the tree

00:00:03.475 --> 00:00:07.642
built by inserting 15, 6, 18

00:00:07.642 --> 00:00:12.008
3, 7, 17, 20, 2, 4

00:00:12.008 --> 00:00:13.893
13 and 9.

00:00:13.893 --> 00:00:15.977
The root is 15

00:00:15.977 --> 00:00:19.152
with children 6 and 18.

00:00:19.152 --> 00:00:23.120
7 has only a right child, 13

00:00:23.120 --> 00:00:25.700
whose left child is 9.

00:00:26.050 --> 00:00:29.389
To search for a key k in the subtree at x

00:00:29.389 --> 00:00:31.456
compare k with x.key.

00:00:31.456 --> 00:00:34.398
If they are equal, we have found it.

00:00:34.398 --> 00:00:35.750
If k is smaller

00:00:35.829 --> 00:00:38.294
then by the BST property it can

00:00:38.373 --> 00:00:40.600
only be in the left subtree

00:00:40.600 --> 00:00:41.951
so search there.

00:00:41.951 --> 00:00:45.370
If it is larger, search the right subtree.

00:00:45.720 --> 00:00:48.911
Each comparison throws away a whole subtree

00:00:48.911 --> 00:00:52.103
as binary search throws away half an array.

00:00:52.103 --> 00:00:55.004
If we fall off the tree, reaching null

00:00:55.004 --> 00:00:56.600
the key is not there.

00:00:57.180 --> 00:00:58.652
Search for 13.

00:00:58.652 --> 00:01:00.049
Start at the root

00:01:00.049 --> 00:01:03.213
13 is less than 15, so go left.

00:01:03.287 --> 00:01:05.788
The whole right subtree of 15

00:01:05.862 --> 00:01:07.260
is out of the game.

00:01:07.610 --> 00:01:11.332
At 6: 13 is larger, so go right.

00:01:11.332 --> 00:01:14.497
3, 2 and 4 are gone too.

00:01:14.497 --> 00:01:17.847
At 7: 13 is larger again

00:01:17.847 --> 00:01:18.777
go right.

00:01:18.777 --> 00:01:20.639
At 13: equal.

00:01:20.639 --> 00:01:21.290
Found.

00:01:21.640 --> 00:01:25.625
The path is 15, 6, 7, 13

00:01:25.625 --> 00:01:28.092
4 nodes out of 11

00:01:28.186 --> 00:01:30.653
and the program SearchDemo

00:01:30.748 --> 00:01:33.120
prints exactly this path.

00:01:33.700 --> 00:01:38.269
In Java, find(x, k) returns the node

00:01:38.381 --> 00:01:42.504
with key k in the subtree x, or null.

00:01:42.504 --> 00:01:46.628
If x is null, or k equals x.key

00:01:46.628 --> 00:01:50.640
return x: that one line covers both

00:01:50.751 --> 00:01:52.980
found and not found.

00:01:53.330 --> 00:01:56.060
If k is less than x.key

00:01:56.060 --> 00:01:59.278
return find(x.left, k).

00:01:59.278 --> 00:02:03.667
Otherwise, return find(x.right, k).

00:02:03.764 --> 00:02:06.885
The code follows the description

00:02:06.982 --> 00:02:08.250
line by line.

00:02:08.830 --> 00:02:10.843
The recursive call is the

00:02:10.924 --> 00:02:12.615
last thing find does.

00:02:12.615 --> 00:02:14.548
That is tail recursion

00:02:14.548 --> 00:02:17.366
and it turns directly into a loop

00:02:17.366 --> 00:02:20.910
while x is not null and k is not x.key

00:02:20.910 --> 00:02:24.292
move x to its left child if k is smaller

00:02:24.292 --> 00:02:27.030
and to its right child otherwise.

00:02:27.380 --> 00:02:29.651
When the loop stops, return x.

00:02:29.651 --> 00:02:32.452
The loop uses no extra stack frames

00:02:32.452 --> 00:02:34.420
it is what libraries use.

00:02:35.000 --> 00:02:36.503
Two more searches.

00:02:36.503 --> 00:02:38.924
17: right at 15

00:02:38.924 --> 00:02:42.514
left at 18, found after three nodes.

00:02:42.514 --> 00:02:46.104
And 15 is found at once, at the root.

00:02:46.104 --> 00:02:49.360
That is the best case: one comparison.

00:02:49.710 --> 00:02:52.750
Now a key that is not in the tree.

00:02:53.330 --> 00:02:56.967
Searching for 5 visits 15, 6

00:02:56.967 --> 00:03:01.165
3 and 4, and then falls off the tree.

00:03:01.165 --> 00:03:03.589
Why does it stop at 4?

00:03:03.683 --> 00:03:06.108
And where would 5 be if

00:03:06.201 --> 00:03:08.066
it were in the tree?

00:03:08.066 --> 00:03:12.170
Pause the video and follow the comparisons.

00:03:15.750 --> 00:03:17.952
5 is less than 15

00:03:17.952 --> 00:03:19.561
and less than 6

00:03:19.561 --> 00:03:23.203
so it would be in the left subtree of 6.

00:03:23.203 --> 00:03:25.490
5 is larger than 3

00:03:25.575 --> 00:03:27.862
so it would be in the right

00:03:27.946 --> 00:03:31.080
subtree of 3, which is node 4.

00:03:31.080 --> 00:03:33.282
5 is larger than 4

00:03:33.282 --> 00:03:36.670
so it would be the right child of 4.

00:03:37.020 --> 00:03:38.438
That child is null

00:03:38.438 --> 00:03:40.528
so 5 is not in the tree.

00:03:40.603 --> 00:03:43.290
And that empty spot is exactly where

00:03:43.365 --> 00:03:46.500
insert(5) will put it, in part three.

00:03:46.850 --> 00:03:49.238
Each step moves one level down

00:03:49.318 --> 00:03:51.627
and does constant work there

00:03:51.627 --> 00:03:55.209
so a search visits at most h + 1 nodes.

00:03:55.209 --> 00:03:56.642
It costs O(h)

00:03:56.642 --> 00:03:58.871
O(1) in the best case

00:03:58.871 --> 00:04:01.180
when the key is at the root

00:04:01.180 --> 00:04:03.170
and O(n) for a chain.

00:04:03.750 --> 00:04:07.173
The smallest key is found by following

00:04:07.263 --> 00:04:11.047
left pointers from the root: 15, 6

00:04:11.047 --> 00:04:14.830
3, 2, until there is no left child.

00:04:15.180 --> 00:04:17.032
Why is 2 the minimum?

00:04:17.032 --> 00:04:18.964
It has no left subtree

00:04:18.964 --> 00:04:21.540
so nothing below it is smaller.

00:04:21.540 --> 00:04:24.760
And every ancestor we passed is larger

00:04:24.760 --> 00:04:27.980
because we went left from each of them.

00:04:28.330 --> 00:04:29.560
Symmetrically

00:04:29.648 --> 00:04:32.459
the largest key is at the end of

00:04:32.547 --> 00:04:35.709
the path of right pointers: 15

00:04:35.709 --> 00:04:37.291
18, 20.

00:04:37.378 --> 00:04:39.838
MinMaxDemo prints

00:04:39.926 --> 00:04:42.210
min = 2 and max = 20.

00:04:42.790 --> 00:04:43.514
In Java

00:04:43.604 --> 00:04:47.314
the findMin method keeps moving x to

00:04:47.404 --> 00:04:50.933
x.left while there is a left child

00:04:50.933 --> 00:04:54.281
and returns the node where it stops.

00:04:54.281 --> 00:04:57.990
findMax does the same with x.right.

00:04:58.340 --> 00:04:59.860
One step per level

00:04:59.860 --> 00:05:02.020
so both take O(h) time.

00:05:02.600 --> 00:05:04.988
Both methods work on any subtree.

00:05:05.060 --> 00:05:06.941
The minimum of the subtree

00:05:07.014 --> 00:05:08.823
at 7 is 7 itself

00:05:08.823 --> 00:05:11.211
because 7 has no left child.

00:05:11.211 --> 00:05:13.960
It does have a right child, 13.

00:05:14.310 --> 00:05:17.594
And the maximum of the subtree at 6

00:05:17.594 --> 00:05:20.619
right to 7, right to 13

00:05:20.619 --> 00:05:22.867
which has no right child.

00:05:22.867 --> 00:05:23.990
So 13.

00:05:24.340 --> 00:05:27.206
So the minimum is not always a leaf.

00:05:27.206 --> 00:05:30.469
It is the first node with no left child

00:05:30.469 --> 00:05:32.936
and it may have a right child.

00:05:32.936 --> 00:05:36.120
And both methods need a non-empty tree

00:05:36.200 --> 00:05:38.190
findMin(null) throws a

00:05:38.269 --> 00:05:40.100
NullPointerException.

00:05:40.680 --> 00:05:43.774
The successor of a node x is the

00:05:43.871 --> 00:05:47.158
node whose key is the smallest key

00:05:47.254 --> 00:05:49.671
larger than x.key.

00:05:49.768 --> 00:05:52.958
That is the node that comes right

00:05:53.055 --> 00:05:57.019
after x in the in-order walk: 2, 3

00:05:57.019 --> 00:06:00.500
4, 6, and so on, up to 20.

00:06:00.500 --> 00:06:04.560
So the successor of 15 is 17.

00:06:04.910 --> 00:06:07.616
The predecessor is the node with the

00:06:07.691 --> 00:06:10.321
largest key smaller than x.key

00:06:10.321 --> 00:06:12.200
the one right before it

00:06:12.200 --> 00:06:15.207
the predecessor of 15 is 13.

00:06:15.207 --> 00:06:18.438
The largest key, 20, has no successor

00:06:18.438 --> 00:06:21.670
and the smallest, 2, has no predecessor.

00:06:22.020 --> 00:06:24.129
We could find these by walking

00:06:24.199 --> 00:06:27.151
the whole tree in order, which is O(n).

00:06:27.221 --> 00:06:30.384
The shape of the tree gives an O(h) method

00:06:30.455 --> 00:06:32.704
that never compares keys at all.

00:06:32.704 --> 00:06:34.180
There are two cases.

00:06:34.760 --> 00:06:37.445
Case 1: x has a right subtree.

00:06:37.529 --> 00:06:39.878
Every key in that subtree is

00:06:39.962 --> 00:06:41.808
larger than x.key.

00:06:41.808 --> 00:06:45.248
And the smallest of them is its minimum

00:06:45.248 --> 00:06:49.024
go right once, then left as far as possible.

00:06:49.107 --> 00:06:51.792
The successor of 15 is

00:06:51.876 --> 00:06:55.400
findMin of the subtree at 18: 17.

00:06:55.750 --> 00:06:58.511
Could a key outside that subtree

00:06:58.597 --> 00:06:59.977
come in between?

00:06:59.977 --> 00:07:00.322
No.

00:07:00.322 --> 00:07:03.773
The larger keys outside it sit above x

00:07:03.859 --> 00:07:06.016
in places where the whole

00:07:06.103 --> 00:07:08.087
subtree of x went left

00:07:08.087 --> 00:07:11.710
so they are larger than everything in it.

00:07:12.060 --> 00:07:13.387
Another example

00:07:13.470 --> 00:07:16.373
the successor of 6 is the minimum

00:07:16.456 --> 00:07:18.447
of the subtree at 7.

00:07:18.447 --> 00:07:20.520
7 has no left child

00:07:20.520 --> 00:07:23.340
so the successor is 7 itself.

00:07:23.920 --> 00:07:26.844
Case 2: x has no right subtree.

00:07:26.844 --> 00:07:27.819
Take 9.

00:07:27.819 --> 00:07:31.008
9 is the left child of 13

00:07:31.097 --> 00:07:33.844
so 13 is larger than 9

00:07:33.932 --> 00:07:36.059
and everything below it

00:07:36.059 --> 00:07:39.160
the successor of 9 is 13.

00:07:39.510 --> 00:07:40.513
Now 13

00:07:40.513 --> 00:07:43.059
which has no right child either.

00:07:43.059 --> 00:07:45.913
Walk up with two pointers: x, and y

00:07:45.913 --> 00:07:46.839
its parent.

00:07:46.839 --> 00:07:49.770
13 is the right child of 7

00:07:49.770 --> 00:07:52.547
so 7 is smaller: keep climbing.

00:07:52.547 --> 00:07:55.093
7 is the right child of 6

00:07:55.093 --> 00:07:56.250
keep climbing.

00:07:56.250 --> 00:07:58.873
6 is the left child of 15.

00:07:58.950 --> 00:08:01.727
So 15 is larger than everything

00:08:01.804 --> 00:08:03.270
we climbed through

00:08:03.270 --> 00:08:06.510
and the successor of 13 is 15.

00:08:06.860 --> 00:08:07.536
In short

00:08:07.536 --> 00:08:10.688
go up until you move up and to the right.

00:08:10.688 --> 00:08:12.940
That parent is the successor.

00:08:13.520 --> 00:08:14.864
What about 20?

00:08:14.864 --> 00:08:17.477
It is the right child of 18

00:08:17.477 --> 00:08:20.240
which is the right child of 15

00:08:20.240 --> 00:08:20.987
the root.

00:08:21.061 --> 00:08:23.301
We reach the root without ever

00:08:23.376 --> 00:08:25.467
coming up from a left child

00:08:25.467 --> 00:08:26.960
and y becomes null.

00:08:27.310 --> 00:08:29.939
Every step went up and to the left

00:08:30.014 --> 00:08:31.968
so 20 is the rightmost

00:08:32.043 --> 00:08:34.747
node of the whole tree: the maximum

00:08:34.747 --> 00:08:36.550
which has no successor.

00:08:37.130 --> 00:08:41.077
In Java: if x.right is not null

00:08:41.077 --> 00:08:45.681
case 1: return findMin(x.right).

00:08:45.681 --> 00:08:50.504
Otherwise, case 2: let y be x.parent.

00:08:50.504 --> 00:08:55.108
While y is not null and x is y.right

00:08:55.108 --> 00:08:57.301
climb: x becomes y

00:08:57.301 --> 00:09:00.370
and y becomes y.parent.

00:09:00.720 --> 00:09:01.912
Then return y.

00:09:01.997 --> 00:09:04.124
It is null exactly when x

00:09:04.209 --> 00:09:06.082
holds the largest key.

00:09:06.082 --> 00:09:09.231
Notice that no key is ever compared

00:09:09.231 --> 00:09:12.720
the shape of the tree does all the work.

00:09:13.300 --> 00:09:16.480
The predecessor is the mirror image

00:09:16.480 --> 00:09:19.307
swap left and right everywhere.

00:09:19.307 --> 00:09:22.399
Case one: if x has a left subtree

00:09:22.399 --> 00:09:25.226
the predecessor is its maximum.

00:09:25.226 --> 00:09:27.523
For fifteen: left to six

00:09:27.523 --> 00:09:30.527
then right to seven and thirteen.

00:09:30.527 --> 00:09:34.060
The predecessor of fifteen is thirteen.

00:09:34.410 --> 00:09:35.969
Case two: otherwise

00:09:36.047 --> 00:09:38.151
climb until you arrive at a

00:09:38.228 --> 00:09:40.254
node from its right child.

00:09:40.254 --> 00:09:41.112
Take nine.

00:09:41.112 --> 00:09:43.917
Nine is the left child of thirteen

00:09:43.917 --> 00:09:45.086
keep climbing.

00:09:45.086 --> 00:09:48.047
Thirteen is the right child of seven

00:09:48.047 --> 00:09:50.930
so the predecessor of nine is seven.

00:09:51.280 --> 00:09:53.891
And two climbs through three and

00:09:53.972 --> 00:09:56.828
six to the root without ever coming

00:09:56.909 --> 00:10:00.254
up from a right child: it is the minimum

00:10:00.254 --> 00:10:01.967
with no predecessor.

00:10:02.049 --> 00:10:04.007
Both methods go down one

00:10:04.088 --> 00:10:05.720
path or up one path.

00:10:06.300 --> 00:10:09.521
SuccessorDemo prints the predecessor and the

00:10:09.594 --> 00:10:12.741
successor of every key of the running tree.

00:10:12.814 --> 00:10:15.156
Fifteen has predecessor thirteen

00:10:15.229 --> 00:10:16.986
and successor seventeen

00:10:16.986 --> 00:10:20.060
and seventeen is the key of the next row.

00:10:20.410 --> 00:10:24.085
Every row's successor is the next row's key

00:10:24.168 --> 00:10:26.089
the successor really is

00:10:26.172 --> 00:10:27.926
next in sorted order.

00:10:28.009 --> 00:10:30.598
Case one goes down one path and

00:10:30.682 --> 00:10:32.853
case two goes up one path

00:10:32.853 --> 00:10:34.690
so both take O(h).

00:10:35.040 --> 00:10:36.329
One more fact

00:10:36.421 --> 00:10:39.644
starting at the minimum and calling

00:10:39.736 --> 00:10:42.866
successor n - 1 times visits

00:10:42.958 --> 00:10:44.984
all the keys in order

00:10:44.984 --> 00:10:47.378
in O(n) time in total

00:10:47.378 --> 00:10:50.600
each edge is walked at most twice.

00:10:51.180 --> 00:10:51.870
To sum up.

00:10:51.870 --> 00:10:53.872
One: search compares the key

00:10:53.941 --> 00:10:55.460
with the current node

00:10:55.529 --> 00:10:57.669
and moves left if it is smaller

00:10:57.738 --> 00:10:59.532
and right if it is larger

00:10:59.601 --> 00:11:01.672
and falling off the tree means

00:11:01.741 --> 00:11:03.191
the key is not there.

00:11:03.191 --> 00:11:05.330
Two: the minimum is at the end

00:11:05.399 --> 00:11:06.849
of the left pointers

00:11:06.918 --> 00:11:08.713
and the maximum at the end

00:11:08.782 --> 00:11:10.300
of the right pointers.

00:11:10.650 --> 00:11:13.517
Three: the successor is the minimum

00:11:13.599 --> 00:11:17.122
of the right subtree, or, if there is none

00:11:17.203 --> 00:11:19.825
the first ancestor that we reach

00:11:19.907 --> 00:11:22.200
coming up from a left child.

00:11:22.200 --> 00:11:24.330
All of them cost O(h).

00:11:24.680 --> 00:11:25.443
Try this.

00:11:25.443 --> 00:11:27.225
In the running tree

00:11:27.225 --> 00:11:29.854
what is the successor of 4

00:11:29.854 --> 00:11:31.720
and which case is it?

00:11:31.720 --> 00:11:33.840
4 has no right child.

00:11:34.190 --> 00:11:38.144
In part 3 we change the tree: insertion

00:11:38.144 --> 00:11:40.750
and deletion in 3 cases.

